6. Gemini AI Testing
Since Gemini is the central component, it requires dedicated testing.

Test different user profiles
Profile A
Beginner + Home + No Equipment
          ↓
Bodyweight exercises

Profile B
Intermediate + Gym
          ↓
Gym-based exercises

Profile C
Beginner + Dumbbells
          ↓
Dumbbell-based exercises

Verify that the generated plan respects:

Fitness goal

Experience level

Available equipment

Number of training days

Requested session duration

User preferences

Structured output testing
The application should verify that Gemini returns the required fields:

planName ✓
durationWeeks ✓
workouts ✓
exercises ✓
sets ✓
reps ✓
restSeconds ✓

If required fields are missing, the application should reject or safely handle the response rather than displaying incomplete data.

7. Safety Testing
Fitness-related AI output should receive additional testing.

Test whether the system:

Avoids diagnosing medical conditions.

Does not claim to replace professional medical advice.

Handles injury/medical-condition information appropriately.

Includes reasonable safety guidance.

Avoids generating obviously unsuitable instructions.

Allows users to stop or replace an unsuitable exercise.

For example:

User reports an injury
          ↓
Gemini
          ↓
System avoids attempting medical diagnosis
          ↓
Provides appropriate safety guidance
          ↓
Encourages professional advice where appropriate

8. Database Testing
Verify:

User records are stored correctly.

Fitness profiles are associated with the correct users.

Workout plans are saved correctly.

Exercises belong to the correct workouts.

Completed workouts are recorded.

Users cannot access another user's private data.

Example:

User A
 ↓
Plan A
 ↓
Workout A

User B
 ↓
Plan B
 ↓
Workout B

User A should never be able to retrieve User B's private workout information.

